## Purpose & Scope

Companion skill to `embedded-code-review` (skill_id: embedded-code-review,
same `implementation-testing` folder) — this skill produces the inputs to
that skill's regression corpus; it does not review code itself.

Keep `eval-cases/` (the regression corpus that checks whether
embedded-code-review's judgment holds up over time — see
`eval-cases-template/README.md` for the schema) growing from real, owned
code rather than external repos, so:

1. No token spend or licensing ambiguity from pulling in unfamiliar
   external codebases.
2. Every case has verifiable ground truth, because the owner actually knows
   what happened in their own history — not a guess about someone else's
   commit.
3. The corpus grows opportunistically alongside real work, rather than
   requiring a dedicated corpus-building effort.

This skill governs *mining*, not *validation*. It produces structured
candidates for the owner to accept, edit, or reject — same governance
principle as skill status changes and as embedded-code-review's own
`HIL_REQUIRED` findings: propose with a stated justification, never assert
silently.

## Trigger Conditions

- User explicitly invokes this skill ("mine eval cases," "refresh the eval
  corpus," "find more concurrency bugs to add as eval cases").
- Immediately after a real bug fix lands in the repo under active work —
  the cheapest validation moment, since the owner still remembers the
  exact defect.
- Periodic sweep (see Refresh Cadence) — monthly, or before any
  embedded-code-review version bump or `draft → validated` transition, per
  the standing eval-hooks process rule.

## Inputs Required

- **Repo path** — the actual local repo to mine (never an external repo,
  never a description of one).
- **Target skill** — which skill's corpus this feeds. Default:
  `embedded-code-review`.
- **Since** — a commit, tag, or date to mine from. Default: since the last
  mining pass, per the state file (see Process, step 2).
- **Category focus** (optional) — narrow to specific `bug_category` values
  (concurrency, shared_resource, cleanup, low_power, fault_tolerance,
  state_variable, general) if the owner wants to fill a known gap rather
  than mine broadly. **If absent, mine broadly** — do not assume a category
  without being told.

## Procedure

This skill is backed by a deterministic script,
`scripts/mine_eval_cases.py` — the mechanical steps (git log, dedup
against the state file, `git show` extraction, file scaffolding) run as
code, not as agent-interpreted instructions, so they execute identically
every time. The agent's job is steps 2 and 4 below (reading results,
drafting prose) — everything else is "run the script, read its output."

### 1. Scan for candidates (read-only)

```bash
python scripts/mine_eval_cases.py scan --repo <path> --system <name> \
  [--since <ref>] [--category <concurrency|shared_resource|cleanup|low_power|fault_tolerance|state_variable>]
```

Writes nothing. Automatically dedupes against every `commit_hash` already
present in `eval-cases/<system>/*/metadata.yaml`, and defaults `--since`
to the last checkpoint in `eval-cases/<system>/.mining-state.yaml` if one
exists (full history otherwise). Prints each new candidate's commit hash,
subject line, and a heuristic `guessed_category` — a starting point, not
a final answer; the heuristic is simple keyword matching and will
sometimes miss (e.g. it may key off an incidental word in the commit
message rather than the actual defect).

### 2. Review candidates with the owner

Present the scan output. For each candidate the owner wants to keep,
identify the relevant file(s) to isolate (prefer the specific file touched
by the fix, not the whole repo) and confirm/correct the category and
target pass.

### 3. Accept — extract and scaffold

```bash
python scripts/mine_eval_cases.py accept --repo <path> --system <name> \
  --commit <hash> --case-id <slug> --file <path-in-repo> \
  --category <category> --target-pass <pass1_intent|pass2_fault_posture|pass3_fit_placement|pass4_regression_coverage> \
  [--criticality <low|medium|high>]
```

Runs `git show <hash>^:<file>` / `git show <hash>:<file>` to write
`before.<ext>` / `after.<ext>` byte-exact from the repo, and scaffolds
`metadata.yaml` (fully filled except the two `expected_verdict_*` fields,
left as `TODO`) and a stub `expected-findings.md` carrying the
`⚠️ MINED CANDIDATE — NOT YET VALIDATED` header. Does **not** advance
the mining checkpoint — a rejected/skipped run shouldn't silently move
past commits the owner hasn't actually seen yet.

### 4. Draft the findings prose

This is the one part that stays agent judgment, not script: fill in the
scaffolded `expected-findings.md`'s Defect / Consequence / Findings
sections from the actual diff, following the convention in
`eval-cases-template/example-case/expected-findings.md`. Write with full
confidence, not hedged — the owner should be reviewing a specific,
checkable claim, not filling in a vague sketch themselves. Also fill the
two `expected_verdict_*` `TODO` fields in `metadata.yaml`.

### 5. Owner validates, then checkpoint

Once the owner has reviewed the drafted findings against the real defect
and confirmed they're accurate, remove the `MINED CANDIDATE` header (or
replace it with a `Validated <date> by owner` line, per the convention
used in the `bus-applications` cases). Only after the **whole batch**
from a scan has been reviewed — accepted cases finalized, rejected ones
explicitly skipped — advance the checkpoint:

```bash
python scripts/mine_eval_cases.py checkpoint --repo <path> --system <name> \
  --commit <newest-hash-seen-in-the-batch> [--source-repo <name>] [--notes "..."]
```

This is a separate, explicit step so a partially-reviewed batch never
silently loses commits the owner hasn't gotten to yet.

## Refresh Cadence

- **Trigger-based (preferred)**: run right after a real bug fix, while
  context is fresh.
- **Periodic sweep**: monthly, or before any embedded-code-review version
  bump / `draft → validated` transition — whichever comes first, per the
  standing eval-hooks process rule.
- **Staleness check** (separate from new mining): periodically confirm
  existing `after.c` cases still match the current state of that code
  region. If the region has been refactored again since the original fix,
  the case may no longer reflect what's actually in the repo — flag
  drifted cases for the owner to refresh or retire, don't leave them
  silently representing dead code paths.

## Decision Rules / Heuristics

- Never write to `eval-cases/` without explicit owner acceptance of that
  specific case — batch acceptance of an entire mining run is not
  sufficient; each case should be individually confirmable, since ground
  truth quality depends on it.
- If a commit's fix is entangled with unrelated changes (a bug fix bundled
  into a larger refactor), prefer skipping it over drafting a noisy case —
  a clean isolated pair is worth more than a large messy one.
- If uncertain about `bug_category` or `target_pass`, say so in the
  candidate summary rather than guessing silently — the owner can correct
  it faster than they can catch a silent miscategorization later.

## Anti-Patterns / Failure Modes

- Writing directly to `eval-cases/` without presenting candidates first.
- Mining external repos "just this once" to fill a category gap faster —
  defeats the purpose (token cost, unverifiable ground truth).
- Treating a mined draft's `expected-findings.md` as validated the moment
  it's written, rather than keeping the `MINED CANDIDATE` marker until the
  owner has actually reviewed it.
- Advancing `.mining-state.yaml`'s checkpoint before the owner has reviewed
  the batch, silently dropping candidates that were never actually
  accepted or rejected.
- Re-scanning full history on every run instead of using the state file —
  wastes time and re-surfaces already-seen candidates.

## Quality Gate

Before this skill moves `draft → validated`:
- [ ] At least one real mining run completed against an actual repo,
      producing at least one accepted case that made it into
      `eval-cases/`.
- [ ] State-file dedup confirmed working (a second run doesn't re-surface
      an already-accepted commit).
- [ ] At least one case where the owner rejected or edited a mined
      candidate, confirming the propose/review split actually holds in
      practice and doesn't get rubber-stamped.
- [ ] Owner sign-off (status change requires explicit user approval per
      project governance — no auto-publish).

